# Copy to .env and fill in. NEVER commit .env -- it holds real credentials.
#   cp .env.example .env && chmod 600 .env          (macOS/Linux)
# The loader reads plain KEY=VALUE lines, ignores '#' comments, and never overrides
# a variable already exported by your shell. Duplicate keys, malformed quoting and
# invalid variable names are rejected with a line number.
#
# Quoting: paste a secret unquoted and it is taken exactly as typed, backslashes
# included -- SIA_CLIENT_SECRET=p@ss\word is the literal p@ss\word. Quote the value
# only if it starts or ends with a space or contains ' #', which otherwise starts a
# comment. Quotes delimit; they do not escape. A backslash is never doubled, and the
# only special sequence inside quotes is a doubled quote, which writes one:
#   SIA_CLIENT_SECRET='p@ss\word'          -> p@ss\word
#   SIA_CLIENT_SECRET='has a # in it'      -> has a # in it
#   SIA_CLIENT_SECRET="it""s quoted"       -> it"s quoted
# Save the file as UTF-8. PowerShell's >, Out-File and Set-Content write UTF-16 by
# default; use Set-Content -Encoding utf8 instead.
# `sia setup` and Settings > Credentials can explicitly save a value here with safe
# quoting; they preserve unrelated lines/comments and set mode 600 on macOS/Linux.
# On Windows, use Setup or Settings to save: SIA creates a private file ACL before
# writing secrets (current user, SYSTEM, Administrators). A manually copied .env
# inherits its folder permissions; `sia doctor` checks its actual Windows ACL.
# If secure saving fails before publication, the terminal home automatically keeps
# the entered credentials in memory for that session and explains what happened.
# `sia settings --show` reports which source wins without displaying secret values.

# --- REQUIRED ---------------------------------------------------------------
# The Identity service user the tool authenticates as. It must be flagged
# "Is OAuth confidential client" and belong to the Secure Infrastructure Access
# administrator role (DpaAdmin), with permission to query Identity directories.
SIA_CLIENT_ID=svc_sia_automation@acme.cyberark.cloud

# Its password. Leave this line out entirely to be prompted instead (no echo) --
# preferable when someone is watching your screen.
SIA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# --- OPTIONAL: PVWA vault stage ---------------------------------------------
# Only needed when [pvwa] base_url is set in config.toml, i.e. when the tool
# should onboard local administrator accounts that are not in the Vault yet.
# This user needs permission to add accounts to the target Safe.
#PVWA_USER=svc_sia_onboarding
#PVWA_PASSWORD=

# --- OPTIONAL: strong-account passwords -------------------------------------
# Only for strong accounts of type=credentials (user name + password stored in
# SIA rather than referenced from the Vault). Vault-referenced accounts never
# need a password here.
#
# The variable name comes from the account's `password_env` column in
# strong_accounts.csv. If that column is left empty, the default name is
# SIA_SA_<NAME>_PASSWORD, where <NAME> is the account name uppercased with every
# non-alphanumeric character replaced by an underscore -- so the account
# "SA-dmz-localadmin" defaults to SIA_SA_SA_DMZ_LOCALADMIN_PASSWORD. Setting
# password_env explicitly (as the example CSV does) is usually clearer.
#
# Beyond a handful of accounts, use a password file instead: a CSV with columns
# name,password, kept outside this repository, passed with --passwords FILE or
# set as [auth] password_file in config.toml.
#SIA_SA_DMZ_LOCALADMIN_PASSWORD=
